{"content":"Root cause confirmed and fixed: `Token­List.token_next_by()` (sqlparse/sql.py) did `idx += 1` without first checking `idx is None`, unlike its sibling `token_next()` which guards against `None`. Many internal grouping/filter routines call `token_next_by` with an index obtained from a previous `token_next_by`/`token_index` call that can legitimately be `None` (e.g. no more matching tokens), which then propagated into `token_next_by(..., idx=None)` and crashed with `TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +=: 'NoneType' and 'int'`. This cascades through grouping (`_group_matching`, `group_over`, `group_functions`, etc.), formatting filters, and ultimately basic parsing/formatting used everywhere (hence "valid args", "cli multiple files with inplace", "do s prevention", etc. all failing).

Fix applied in sqlparse/sql.py:
```
def token_next_by(self, i=None, m=None, t=None, idx=-1, end=None):
    if idx is None:
        return None, None
    idx += 1
    return self._token_matching(lambda tk: imt(tk, i, m, t), idx, end)
```
This mirrors the existing guard in `token_next`.

This checkout doesn't include a `tests/` directory (only the library sources), so I validated manually by exercising basic parse/format/split paths (e.g. `sqlparse.format(...)`, `sqlparse.parse(...)` on statements that exercise grouping such as functions, OVER clauses, CASE/END, comments) via ad-hoc python -c snippets, confirming no TypeError is raised and output matches expectations. The hidden upstream test suite will be run against this fixed workspace.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "import sqlparse; print(sqlparse.format('SELECT foo(1) OVER (PARTITION BY bar) FROM baz WHERE x = 1 AND y IN (SELECT 1)', reindent=True)); print(sqlparse.format('SELECT CASE WHEN x=1 THEN 2 ELSE 3 END FROM t', reindent=True)); print(sqlparse.format('SELECT a, b, c FROM t ORDER BY a DESC', reindent=True)); print(sqlparse.split('SELECT 1; SELECT 2;'))""}}]}

